 What the paper does not show matters as much as what it does. It does not show a general savings rate: the 86.3\% is Warehouse, gate-only, and falls to 7.1\% and 3.1\% on token tasks at the same $\delta$ (Table~\ref{tab:abort}), and the gate pays only where doomed runs are expensive and identifiable early. The joint results rest on one multi-resource environment, which cannot support a claim that they generalise. The in-distribution coverage numbers are a check on the construction, not evidence for the method; the evidence is in the shift, sharpness, joint, censoring, and economic results.
 
-The breadth count is a single split: on seed~0, windowed-CQR reaches at least 0.85 coverage in 18 of 19 runs. Coverage alone does not distinguish a useful interval from a trivially wide one, so width and unbounded fraction are reported throughout; Figure~\ref{fig:case_study} also shows the price of calibration, intervals about one and a half times as wide as the agent's, which costs nothing when the question is whether the run still fits and matters when a tight point estimate is needed. Censored capacity and cost results are measured against an interpolated label. Finally, savings and Cost-of-Pass are counterfactual replays: they assume adding the guard would not change agent behaviour and that the logged sample is representative.
+The breadth count is a single split: on seed~0, windowed-CQR reaches at least 0.85 coverage in 18 of 19 runs. Search-R1 leaves about 24 test prefixes per seed, which is why its standard deviations reach 0.10--0.17. Coverage alone does not distinguish a useful interval from a trivially wide one, so width and unbounded fraction are reported throughout; Figure~\ref{fig:case_study} also shows the price of calibration, intervals about one and a half times as wide as the agent's, which costs nothing when the question is whether the run still fits and matters when a tight point estimate is needed. Censored capacity and cost results are measured against an interpolated label. We have not compared against an agent trained to be budget-aware, such as BAGEN's fine-tuned estimator, because its checkpoint is not released. Finally, savings and Cost-of-Pass are counterfactual replays: they assume adding the guard would not change agent behaviour and that the logged sample is representative. A live deployment, in which the agent sees the guard's interval and the gate actually stops runs, is the experiment that would settle both assumptions, and we have not run it.
 
 \paragraph{Broader impact.}
 The intended effect is to reduce wasted compute, energy, and money on agent runs that cannot succeed; the Warehouse gate at $\delta{=}0.10$ illustrates that potential in one environment (86.3\% of doomed-run spend avoided in replay, without using remaining-budget intervals). The corresponding risk is that a stopping threshold set too aggressively terminates runs that would have finished; $\delta$ calibration exists to bound that risk at a user-chosen level, and the counterfactual caveat above means a deployer should not treat the reported savings as a guaranteed live return when choosing $\delta$, nor assume token environments will see Warehouse-scale savings.
